    "1/1": "Based on the report, the `DangerButton` is never announcing its \u201cdanger\u201d state to assistive technologies because it neither exposes an ARIA descriptor nor embeds any hidden \u201cDanger!\u201d text. All other variants simply render the base `<Button>` and inherit its behavior; the fix is to wrap `DangerButton`\u2019s children with a visually hidden span and hook it up via `aria-describedby`.\n\nHere is a single PATCH to `packages/react/src/components/DangerButton/DangerButton.js`:\n\n```javascript\n### packages/react/src/components/DangerButton/DangerButton.js\n<<<<<<< SEARCH\nconst DangerButton = (props) => <Button kind=\"danger\" {...props} />;\n=======\nconst DangerButton = (props) => (\n  <Button\n    kind=\"danger\"\n    aria-describedby=\"danger-info\"\n    {...props}>\n    {props.children}\n    <span\n      id=\"danger-info\"\n      className=\"bx--visually-hidden\">\n      Danger!\n    </span>\n  </Button>\n);\n>>>>>>> REPLACE\n```\n\nExplanation of the change:\n\n- SEARCH locates the one\u2010liner `<Button kind=\"danger\" {...props} />`.\n- REPLACE expands it into a JSX block that:\n  1. Adds `aria-describedby=\"danger-info\"` onto the button so screen readers will read the hidden content.\n  2. Includes a `<span id=\"danger-info\" className=\"bx--visually-hidden\">Danger!</span>` as the first child, providing the required \u201cDanger!\u201d cue to assistive technologies without affecting the visual UI."
}